many of you probably have seen over the  last 24 to 48 hours that there are  reports circulating that the united  states and the irs are not going to tax  crypto gains that are derived from  staking or mining well before we get too  into this this may not actually be  exactly true so let's dig into exactly  what's happening here first there's a  couple in nashville tennessee the  jarretts they have requested a refund of  3 293 of income tax that they previously  paid in 2019 after they received 8 800  tezos tokens the couple is also seeking  a 500 increase in tax credits for lost  income the irs and the courts have been  looking at this case in detail the  reason why is that these tokens were  obtained through proof of stake  protocols those are taxpayer-created  property according to the jarretts and  taxpayer created property is not  actually supposed to be taxed until it  is sold or exchanged and so ultimately  what the government the irs the courts  etc are all going to have to decide is  how are they going to treat these  staking tokens well the irs decided that  they should refund the taxes related to  those unsold tasos tokens that refund  would be a reward that could have proven  exactly what the jarrods are claiming  but the plaintiffs rejected that refund  and they want a formal policy statement  obviously they are spending a lot of  money and it's not simply to recoup  three thousand dollars or to get 500  worth of lost income they're trying to  force the hand of the irs and the  government and the courts to say if we  stake and actually receive tokens from  staking or if we mine tokens and do not  sell or exchange them do we have to pay  taxes on them or not this would be a  massive massive boom to the industry if  all of a sudden every single miner could  say i don't have to pay taxes on what i  mine until i either sell the tokens or i  exchange them for another one same could  be said for proof of stake blockchains  as well  now it is important to understand the  reason why i say that the mainstream  narrative that the irs will not tax  crypto in 2022 may not exactly be true  is because the irs is simply trying to  offer a refund here there has been no  policy updated there has been no  definitive agreement yet the hope of the  industry is that that is coming but we  do not have it yet and so ultimately  what we are all waiting for is to see  where this case ends up if the jareds  prevail and the irs says that proof of  stake tokens that you accumulate and do  not sell or exchange or tokens that are  accumulated through the mining process  that you do not sell or exchange there  is no taxation on it all of a sudden i  think you'll see it an absolute tailwind  for those types of activities in the  crypto industry there's billions of  dollars of value being created and if  you don't have to pay income tax you  don't have to pay capital gains tax  until you sell or exchange them that's  going to be a really really big deal
 

